1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,335
The author is certain: the branch adds a
report writer and never opened the config

2
00:00:09,375 --> 00:00:14,151
file. The reviewer is equally certain:
there is a red line in the diff, a setting

3
00:00:14,191 --> 00:00:16,172
called NIGHTLY_PASS_MARK, removed.

4
00:00:16,512 --> 00:00:21,245
The reviewer ran git diff main feat/report.
The pull request page shows something

5
00:00:21,285 --> 00:00:26,136
else. Both are diffs of "the branch against
main". They differ by one dot, and that

6
00:00:26,176 --> 00:00:28,788
dot decides which two
snapshots are compared.

7
00:00:29,128 --> 00:00:34,057
The same two characters also appear in git
log, where they mean something different

8
00:00:34,097 --> 00:00:38,607
again. Today you sort that out for good.
So who was right, the author or the

9
00:00:38,647 --> 00:00:40,224
reviewer? Keep your answer.

10
00:00:42,064 --> 00:00:46,144
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:46,184 --> 00:00:51,142
For twenty videos you've been typing
things like main..HEAD, HEAD~2, @{u},

12
00:00:51,182 --> 00:00:56,681
ORIG_HEAD, feat/x@{1}. Each one names a
commit, one saved snapshot of the project,

13
00:00:56,721 --> 00:01:01,680
or a set of commits. This video collects
them into a system, because every

14
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:06,070
investigation command in the next
module takes them as arguments.

15
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:12,395
Three parts. First, naming one commit: by
ancestry, by reflog entry, by upstream, by

16
00:01:12,435 --> 00:01:18,348
message search, by path. Second, naming
sets of commits: what A..B and A...B select

17
00:01:18,388 --> 00:01:24,014
in git log. Third, what git diff does
with the same text, which is not the same

18
00:01:24,054 --> 00:01:27,959
thing, and which of the two
forms a pull request shows.

19
00:01:28,299 --> 00:01:33,114
The method for all the range exercises today
is the same: draw the graph, shade the

20
00:01:33,154 --> 00:01:35,513
commits you expect, then run the command.

21
00:01:35,853 --> 00:01:40,698
The project is scorekit, a small library
that scores model answers against reference

22
00:01:40,738 --> 00:01:45,466
answers. Its history was prepared for the
investigation chapter, and you'll use it

23
00:01:45,506 --> 00:01:47,211
through the whole next module.

24
00:01:49,051 --> 00:01:50,629
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:50,969 --> 00:01:52,381
After this video you can:

26
00:01:52,721 --> 00:01:58,260
Name a commit by ancestry, by reflog entry,
by upstream, by message search and by

27
00:01:58,300 --> 00:01:58,604
path.

28
00:01:58,944 --> 00:02:02,624
State what A..B and
A...B select in git log.

29
00:02:02,964 --> 00:02:08,534
State what A..B and A...B compare in git
diff, and why the dots mean something else

30
00:02:08,574 --> 00:02:08,939
there.

31
00:02:09,279 --> 00:02:12,351
Choose the diff form that matches
what a pull request shows.

32
00:02:12,691 --> 00:02:15,282
Predict log and diff
output from a drawn graph.

33
00:02:17,122 --> 00:02:18,200
Naming one commit.

34
00:02:18,540 --> 00:02:22,421
Read the third column. It tells
you how far to trust each name.

35
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:26,902
Names read from the graph and from
objects mean the same in every clone.

36
00:02:26,942 --> 00:02:31,632
Names read from your reflog, the local
list of where each ref has been, mean

37
00:02:31,672 --> 00:02:35,989
something only in your repository.
@{u} depends on your configuration.

38
00:02:36,329 --> 00:02:41,309
Naming sets of commits. In one sentence:
a range is a set of commits defined by

39
00:02:41,349 --> 00:02:46,584
reachability: everything you can reach from
the included tips, minus everything you

40
00:02:46,624 --> 00:02:51,159
can reach from the excluded ones.
Reaching means following parent links.

41
00:02:51,199 --> 00:02:56,307
On screen, today's graph: main has two
commits of its own, feat/report has three,

42
00:02:56,347 --> 00:03:00,692
and ca7e2b7 is their merge base, the
most recent commit both contain.

43
00:03:01,032 --> 00:03:05,200
For history-walking commands, one
revision means "this commit and all its

44
00:03:05,240 --> 00:03:09,985
ancestors", and several revisions mean the
union. A leading caret excludes a commit

45
00:03:10,025 --> 00:03:12,925
and its ancestors. The
dotted forms are shorthands.

46
00:03:13,265 --> 00:03:18,645
An omitted side means HEAD:
origin/main.. is origin/main..HEAD.

47
00:03:18,985 --> 00:03:24,020
Diff. In one sentence: git diff 🟢 SAFE
compares exactly two snapshots, and every

48
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:27,508
form of the command differs
only in which two it picks.

49
00:03:27,848 --> 00:03:32,402
The manual is blunt about the dots: "diff
is about comparing two endpoints, not

50
00:03:32,442 --> 00:03:37,111
ranges", and the range notations do not
mean a range as defined in the section on

51
00:03:37,151 --> 00:03:41,995
specifying ranges. The textbook adds that
the two meanings are among the most common

52
00:03:42,035 --> 00:03:46,530
misreadings of Git. So if the dots have
confused you, you're in large company.

53
00:03:46,870 --> 00:03:52,051
So, the two sentences to keep. In git log,
two dots mean "reachable from B and not

54
00:03:52,091 --> 00:03:57,272
from A", and three dots mean the symmetric
difference: reachable from one of them,

55
00:03:57,312 --> 00:04:02,238
not from both. In git diff, two dots
compare the two endpoints, and three dots

56
00:04:02,278 --> 00:04:04,148
compare the merge base with B.

57
00:04:04,488 --> 00:04:09,779
Inside .git, none of this writes anything.
Every command in this video is 🟢 SAFE.

58
00:04:11,619 --> 00:04:16,810
The textbook's analogy for ranges: colour
every commit reachable from B green, then

59
00:04:16,850 --> 00:04:20,213
colour every commit reachable
from A red, red winning.

60
00:04:20,253 --> 00:04:21,789
A..B is what stays green.

61
00:04:22,129 --> 00:04:26,597
Try it on the picture, with main as A
and feat/report as B: three commits stay

62
00:04:26,637 --> 00:04:26,944
green.

63
00:04:27,284 --> 00:04:31,709
The picture breaks if you think of "between":
a range is not an interval on a line,

64
00:04:31,749 --> 00:04:36,120
and with merges in the graph the green set
can contain commits made long before A.

65
00:04:36,460 --> 00:04:40,917
For diff, the analogy is two photographs.
A diff is the comparison of two

66
00:04:40,957 --> 00:04:45,906
photographs. It's not the film between
them: whatever happened in between, and in

67
00:04:45,946 --> 00:04:51,079
which order, is invisible. It breaks at one
form. A...B chooses its first photograph

68
00:04:51,119 --> 00:04:56,068
by consulting the commit graph, so the
same two branch names can give a different

69
00:04:56,108 --> 00:04:59,702
diff next week, after one of
them has moved or been merged.

70
00:05:00,042 --> 00:05:04,129
One sentence joins the two: log selects
commits, diff selects two trees.

71
00:05:04,169 --> 00:05:08,772
If you catch yourself asking "which commits
does this diff cover", you have mixed

72
00:05:08,812 --> 00:05:09,403
the models.

73
00:05:11,243 --> 00:05:16,207
One graph, for both halves: the log ranges
select sets of commits on it, and the

74
00:05:16,247 --> 00:05:20,336
diff forms compare two of its trees.
Keep it in mind for the demo.

75
00:05:22,176 --> 00:05:23,859
Part 1: naming one commit.

76
00:05:24,199 --> 00:05:29,496
HEAD~1, HEAD~2, and HEAD^, which
is the same commit as HEAD~1.

77
00:05:29,536 --> 00:05:34,919
The difference between the tilde
and the caret shows on merges.

78
00:05:34,959 --> 00:05:39,482
8657273 is a merge commit,
a commit with two parents.

79
00:05:39,522 --> 00:05:44,474
Quick quiz, three options.
Which two name the same commit?

80
00:05:44,514 --> 00:05:50,500
Option one: ^1 and ~1. Option two:
~1 and ^2. Option three: ^1 and ^2.

81
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:51,877
Say it out loud.

82
00:06:02,217 --> 00:06:07,869
Option one. ^1 and ~1 are the same commit,
the first parent, which is the branch

83
00:06:07,909 --> 00:06:13,846
that was merged into. On screen that's
389337a. ^2 is the tip of the branch that was

84
00:06:13,886 --> 00:06:18,258
merged in, c0d33a5, and ^2~1
walks one step back on that side.

85
00:06:18,298 --> 00:06:24,021
The caret chooses among parents. The tilde
walks generations along first parents.

86
00:06:24,361 --> 00:06:28,203
The reflog forms read your
local reflog and nothing else.

87
00:06:28,243 --> 00:06:32,221
main@{yesterday} does not mean
"the commit made yesterday".

88
00:06:32,261 --> 00:06:37,534
It means "what main pointed at in this
clone 24 hours ago", here a commit from

89
00:06:37,574 --> 00:06:42,642
Monday 12:31, because the lab clock
stands at Tuesday shortly before 13:00.

90
00:06:42,682 --> 00:06:48,090
On a teammate's machine, or in a fresh
clone whose reflog starts today, the same

91
00:06:48,130 --> 00:06:53,607
expression gives a different answer or a
warning. Use reflog forms to investigate

92
00:06:53,647 --> 00:06:58,987
your own repository. Use dates with git
log --since to investigate the project.

93
00:06:59,327 --> 00:07:03,797
@{u} resolves through the upstream
configuration: the branch on the server that

94
00:07:03,837 --> 00:07:08,478
yours follows. @{u}.. is then "my commits
that the remote-tracking branch does not

95
00:07:08,518 --> 00:07:08,820
have".

96
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:14,459
Searching by message. :/nightly run matches
two commits in this repository, the

97
00:07:14,499 --> 00:07:19,797
original on feat/report and its copy on
main. The rule "youngest, from any ref"

98
00:07:19,837 --> 00:07:25,000
picked the copy. <rev>^{/<text>} restricts
the search to the ancestors of one

99
00:07:25,040 --> 00:07:27,906
revision and is the
form to use in scripts.

100
00:07:28,246 --> 00:07:33,253
An annotated tag is an object of its own,
so the tag name and the commit it marks

101
00:07:33,293 --> 00:07:38,363
have different IDs: the first line is the
tag object, the second and third are the

102
00:07:38,403 --> 00:07:43,597
commit, the fourth is its tree. ^{} peels.
You need the suffix when you compare IDs,

103
00:07:43,637 --> 00:07:46,775
as in "is the tag still on
the commit we deployed".

104
00:07:47,115 --> 00:07:53,612
Paths select inside a revision: a blob in
main, the same path in v0.1.0, and with a

105
00:07:53,652 --> 00:07:59,992
leading colon alone, the index entry. git
show v0.1.0:scorekit/config.py prints a

106
00:08:00,032 --> 00:08:03,221
file from a release without any checkout.

107
00:08:03,561 --> 00:08:08,984
And when a name resolves to nothing, Git
says so: a path that did not exist at that

108
00:08:09,024 --> 00:08:11,353
revision, a tag that does not exist.

109
00:08:11,693 --> 00:08:17,145
Part 2: ranges in git log. labs/run
ch14a/ranges. Use the diagram.

110
00:08:17,185 --> 00:08:23,885
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: draw
this graph, and for main..feat/report,

111
00:08:23,925 --> 00:08:26,298
shade the commits you expect.

112
00:08:28,638 --> 00:08:33,841
Three commits, and three spellings of the
same set: two dots, a leading caret, and

113
00:08:33,881 --> 00:08:34,224
--not.

114
00:08:34,564 --> 00:08:36,134
The mirror image: two commits.

115
00:08:36,474 --> 00:08:39,568
Now three dots. Shade first. I'll wait.

116
00:08:44,408 --> 00:08:46,408
Five commits: the union of the two.

117
00:08:46,748 --> 00:08:52,698
--left-right says which side each commit is
on, and --boundary adds the commit where

118
00:08:52,738 --> 00:08:56,977
the walk stopped, marked with
an o: the merge base, ca7e2b7.

119
00:08:57,317 --> 00:09:02,345
The parent shorthands, on the
merge commit. ^@ is both parents.

120
00:09:02,385 --> 00:09:08,860
^! is the commit alone. And 8657273^- is
the answer to "what did this merge bring

121
00:09:08,900 --> 00:09:13,767
in": the merge itself and the
two commits of the side branch.

122
00:09:14,107 --> 00:09:19,885
For an ordinary commit, A^! gives git
diff the pair that git show uses.

123
00:09:20,225 --> 00:09:25,867
An empty A..B means B is an ancestor
of A, which is the test git merge-base

124
00:09:25,907 --> 00:09:27,609
--is-ancestor performs.

125
00:09:27,949 --> 00:09:32,822
And one query lists all unmerged work
in a repository: --branches --not main.

126
00:09:32,862 --> 00:09:38,181
Look at the last three lines of that list.
feat/rouge-l appears although its content

127
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:43,222
is on main: it was squash-merged, and a
squash creates a new commit that has no

128
00:09:43,262 --> 00:09:47,369
parent link to the branch. The
graph knows ancestry, not content.

129
00:09:47,409 --> 00:09:50,814
You've met that sentence in
four different videos now.

130
00:09:51,154 --> 00:09:57,289
Part 3: the same dots in git diff.
labs/run ch14a/diff-endpoints.

131
00:09:57,629 --> 00:10:02,676
The graph of the diagram. The branch has
three commits of its own, main has two, and

132
00:10:02,716 --> 00:10:07,037
the merge base is ca7e2b7. Two endpoints,
with and without the two dots:

133
00:10:07,377 --> 00:10:13,045
Identical. Three files, and one of them
is scorekit/config.py with a deletion.

134
00:10:13,085 --> 00:10:17,289
Now three dots. Predict which
files are listed. I'll wait.

135
00:10:22,129 --> 00:10:25,938
config.py is gone from the list,
and README.md has appeared.

136
00:10:25,978 --> 00:10:30,427
The second command spells the same
comparison out with git merge-base.

137
00:10:30,467 --> 00:10:35,623
The two results disagree about two files,
and each disagreement is worth reading.

138
00:10:41,963 --> 00:10:46,237
That's the opening story. feat/report
never touched scorekit/config.py.

139
00:10:46,277 --> 00:10:51,099
main added a line after the fork. Going
from the tree of main to the tree of the

140
00:10:51,139 --> 00:10:55,839
branch, that line is absent, so the
two-endpoint diff prints it as a deletion.

141
00:10:55,879 --> 00:11:00,761
The reviewer compared tips and read main's
own progress, reversed, as part of the

142
00:11:00,801 --> 00:11:04,103
feature. Almost every reviewer
types that command once.

143
00:11:04,143 --> 00:11:05,744
With three dots: no output.

144
00:11:12,284 --> 00:11:16,804
The README paragraph is the opposite case.
It was written on the branch and then

145
00:11:16,844 --> 00:11:20,794
cherry-picked to main. Both tips
contain it, so the tips don't differ.

146
00:11:20,834 --> 00:11:25,411
The merge base does not contain it, so the
three-dot diff shows it as a change of

147
00:11:25,451 --> 00:11:27,748
the branch, although main already has it.

148
00:11:28,088 --> 00:11:32,638
Swap the operands and the three-dot form
compares the merge base with the other tip:

149
00:11:32,678 --> 00:11:34,223
what main did since the fork.

150
00:11:34,563 --> 00:11:40,332
One more form, useful in forensics: the
operands may be trees or blobs, addressed as

151
00:11:40,372 --> 00:11:41,231
<rev>:<path>.

152
00:11:41,571 --> 00:11:45,736
A directory compared between a release
tag and main, and then one file.

153
00:11:45,776 --> 00:11:47,039
No checkout is needed.

154
00:11:48,878 --> 00:11:50,527
Five mistakes to watch for.

155
00:11:50,867 --> 00:11:55,890
Reviewing a branch with git diff main feature.
Root cause: two endpoints compare the

156
00:11:55,930 --> 00:12:00,954
two tips, so everything main gained after
the fork appears reversed, as deletions by

157
00:12:00,994 --> 00:12:01,677
the feature.

158
00:12:02,017 --> 00:12:07,569
Reading A..B in git diff as a range of
commits. Root cause: diff compares two trees;

159
00:12:07,609 --> 00:12:11,031
the dots are a spelling of
two endpoints, not a set.

160
00:12:11,371 --> 00:12:15,191
Thinking of A..B in git log as
"the commits between A and B".

161
00:12:15,231 --> 00:12:20,126
Root cause: a range is defined by
reachability, and with merges it can contain

162
00:12:20,166 --> 00:12:21,455
commits older than A.

163
00:12:21,795 --> 00:12:25,860
Using main@{yesterday} in a script
or in a message to a colleague.

164
00:12:25,900 --> 00:12:31,022
Root cause: reflog forms read the local
reflog; the same text names another commit,

165
00:12:31,062 --> 00:12:32,826
or nothing, in another clone.

166
00:12:33,166 --> 00:12:38,762
Using :/text to find "the" commit. Root
cause: it returns the youngest match

167
00:12:38,802 --> 00:12:44,398
reachable from any ref, which may be a
copy on another branch; <rev>^{/text}

168
00:12:44,438 --> 00:12:45,955
restricts the search.

169
00:12:47,795 --> 00:12:52,399
Now, out of the lab. Three habits from
the textbook, for a team that reviews and

170
00:12:52,439 --> 00:12:52,922
releases.

171
00:12:53,262 --> 00:12:57,830
Review a branch with three dots: "what does
this branch introduce", independent of

172
00:12:57,870 --> 00:13:02,495
what the target did meanwhile. Use two
endpoints for "how do these two trees differ

173
00:13:02,535 --> 00:13:06,205
right now", for example a release
tag against the deployed commit.

174
00:13:06,545 --> 00:13:12,846
Before a push, git log --oneline origin/main..HEAD
is "what am I about to publish".

175
00:13:12,886 --> 00:13:19,187
After a fetch, git log --oneline HEAD..origin/main
is "what will a merge bring me".

176
00:13:19,527 --> 00:13:24,159
And in scripts, write the explicit --merge-base
spelling instead of three dots, so

177
00:13:24,199 --> 00:13:28,718
that the next reader does not have to
remember which meaning the dots have here.

178
00:13:29,058 --> 00:13:33,796
The "Files changed" tab of a pull request,
GitHub's proposal to merge one branch

179
00:13:33,836 --> 00:13:38,634
into another, shows a three-dot diff, from
the merge base to the head of the pull

180
00:13:38,674 --> 00:13:41,739
request branch, according
to GitHub's documentation.

181
00:13:41,779 --> 00:13:46,636
That's why a pull request whose branch is
behind its base does not show the base's

182
00:13:46,676 --> 00:13:51,653
newer commits as deletions, and why a change
that reached the base by another route,

183
00:13:51,693 --> 00:13:56,610
as the README paragraph did, still appears
in it until the branch is merged with or

184
00:13:56,650 --> 00:13:57,924
rebased onto the base.

185
00:13:58,264 --> 00:14:02,895
So who was right, the author or the reviewer?
Both were right about what they saw.

186
00:14:02,935 --> 00:14:05,743
They were looking at
different pairs of snapshots.

187
00:14:07,583 --> 00:14:13,660
Your turn. Do Lab 10.4, "Predict two-dot
and three-dot output for git log and for

188
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:17,209
git diff", in
lab-manual/m10-range-notation.md.

189
00:14:17,549 --> 00:14:22,092
The lab gives you a graph. For each of
the four commands, before you run it:

190
00:14:22,432 --> 00:14:26,392
For the two git log forms: shade the
selected commits on your drawing and write

191
00:14:26,432 --> 00:14:27,151
their subjects.

192
00:14:27,491 --> 00:14:32,559
For the two git diff forms: write which
two commits' trees are compared, and list

193
00:14:32,599 --> 00:14:34,514
the files you expect in --stat.

194
00:14:34,854 --> 00:14:39,740
Then predict how each of the four outputs
changes after the branch is updated from

195
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:40,281
its base.

196
00:14:40,621 --> 00:14:47,027
The challenge is Exercise 10.4, Level 2,
"ranges on a history with a merge", in

197
00:14:47,067 --> 00:14:49,720
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

198
00:14:51,560 --> 00:14:57,369
Q394: "What is the difference between ..
and ..., in git log and in git diff?"

199
00:14:59,709 --> 00:15:04,873
Answer out loud first. There are four cells,
and a strong answer fills all four with

200
00:15:04,913 --> 00:15:10,015
precise wording: for log, in terms of
reachability, and for diff, in terms of which

201
00:15:10,055 --> 00:15:15,033
two trees. It says why the same notation
has two meanings, by saying what kind of

202
00:15:15,073 --> 00:15:20,113
thing each command operates on. It gives
one practical consequence for review, and

203
00:15:20,153 --> 00:15:25,256
names what a pull request page shows. Drawing
a small diverged graph while you talk

204
00:15:25,296 --> 00:15:28,663
is not a crutch here. It's
the fastest way to be exact.

205
00:15:30,503 --> 00:15:34,630
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

206
00:15:34,970 --> 00:15:40,239
A commit can be named through the graph
with ~ and ^, through my reflog with @{n},

207
